# Exact recorded fidelity on the native encoding grid

Development version 0.1.0, 21 September 2026. Status: exploratory.

Both implementations support the unchanged 0.05 m/s recorded-fidelity requirement
on all five exposed tail parts using **69 logical reveals each** (16, 11, 16, 15,
11). The earlier result remains 90 versus 89 under its different relaxed models.
The current equal-information comparator gains the same benefit; no Reiyah
decision-quality or acquisition advantage is established.

The correction preserves original velocity constraints, fixed native interpolation
knots and finite integer encoding. It computes tight minimum/maximum errors,
attaining complete native trajectories and complete obtainable reply classes.
The coarse baseline can be off lattice: all five initial minimum errors are
0.00005 m/s, not zero. The conventional arm independently uses integer path
propagation and exact feasibility search. Both share one author, parser,
source preparation and workflow shell.

Thirteen authored cases retain six supported, three contradicted, three
inconsistent-premise and one unavailable/unresolved result per arm. They need
eight logical reveals per arm. Main runs execute 18 cases and 77 reveals per arm.
An encodable reply outside the current family is inconsistent premises.
An unencodable reply is a source refusal.

The separate checker replays 190 model/witness stages and 154 complete reply
partitions across both arms. Direct enumeration covers 165 small-domain contracts,
545 feasible world instances across those contracts and 384 arm/query partitions.
Twenty-four refusal checks and twelve actual-output mutations pass.
These are correctness checks, not independent scientific replication.

The selected reference's maximum actual stored-data error remains 0.0042 m/s.
Its five parts share one recording and are dependent. This exposed development
replay does not estimate an error rate, validate physical clearance, justify a
customer/safety threshold or establish frontier performance. Zero physical cases
ran. The original 5 m/s² premise holds over the selected stored intervals,
but it is not a calibrated physical acceleration guarantee.

Whole arm processes took about 0.407 s each. Equal-scope internal workflow times
were 0.2501 s for Reiyah and 0.2447 s for conventional. One fixed-order run is not
a speed benchmark. Full input was loaded: logical reveals are not hardware
acquisition savings. Full engineering effort, charges, energy, network overhead
and unsampled peaks remain unknown. See [costs](costs.json).

